          Updated September 8, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
        </div>
        <a href="#what-to-verify" class="btn-ghost">What to Verify</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We research every product on this page independently. If you buy through our links, we may earn a
        commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Learn more about how we review.</a>
      </p>
      <!-- Editorial draft banner -->
      <div class="ed-draft">
        <p class="m-0 mb-2"><strong>Editorial draft — not yet published.</strong> The framing and analysis below are complete. The following need verified manufacturer data
          before this page goes live, and nothing on this page should be treated as factual until they
          are filled in:</p>
        <ul class="m-0 pl-5">
          {DATA_PENDING.map((item) => (
            <li>{item}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          This guide is deliberately structured around questions rather than answers. Treadmill
          brand pages that assert specifications they have not verified are worse than useless,
          because they read as authoritative — so what follows is the framework we apply to any
          Horizon machine, with the model-specific figures marked as pending.
        </p>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#reading-a-lineup">How to Read a Treadmill Lineup</a></li>
            <li><a href="#what-to-verify">The Seven Things to Verify Before Buying</a></li>
            <li><a href="#subscription-question">The Subscription Question</a></li>
            <li><a href="#warranty-structure">Reading a Warranty Properly</a></li>
            <li><a href="#cross-shopping">What to Cross-Shop Against</a></li>
            <li><a href="#ownership">What Ownership Looks Like, Whatever You Buy</a></li>
            <li><a href="#what-is-pending">What This Page Still Needs</a></li>
            <li><a href="#verdict">How to Use This Page Today</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="reading-a-lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Read a Treadmill Lineup</h2>
<p>
            Treadmill model numbers are not designed to be informative to shoppers, and Horizon is
            no exception. Before comparing any two machines in a lineup, establish three things.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What the number encodes.</strong> Most manufacturers use a series prefix and a
            tier number, where the prefix indicates the product family and the number indicates
            position within it. What that does not tell you is whether two machines share a frame,
            a motor or a deck — which is the thing that actually determines how they compare.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Where the model-year boundary falls.</strong> Annual refreshes in this category
            are overwhelmingly console and software changes, with frame, motor, deck and rollers
            carried over unchanged. That makes the outgoing model year frequently the best value in
            any lineup, and it makes "the latest model" a weak reason to pay more. Ask the retailer
            explicitly what changed between years, and treat a vague answer as evidence that the
            answer is the screen.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Which retailer-specific variants exist.</strong> The same chassis is often sold
            under different model numbers at different retailers, occasionally with a genuine
            specification difference and more often without. This is the single most common source
            of confusion when comparing prices across sellers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-verify" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Seven Things to Verify Before Buying</h2>
<p>
            These are the specifications that determine whether a machine suits you, listed in the
            order we would establish them. Every one of them should come from the manufacturer or
            the retailer in writing, for the exact model and model year you are buying.
          </p>
          <p>
            One machine in the lineup has already been through this process and has a published
            review: the <a href="/horizon-t101-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon T101</a>,
            whose figures were cross-checked against the manufacturer listing, retailer listings and
            independent coverage. Use it as the worked example of what the seven questions below
            look like when they have been answered.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>One: deck length and width.</strong> Sixty inches is the running threshold for
            anyone over about 5'8"; 55 is walking and light jogging. Ask for the usable flat deck
            measurement rather than the belt figure, which runs one to two inches longer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Two: continuous-duty horsepower.</strong> Not peak. If only a peak figure is
            quoted, the continuous rating is unflattering.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Three: roller diameter.</strong> The least advertised and most predictive
            longevity specification. Budget machines use 1.9 inches, good ones 2.5, commercial
            3 or more.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Four: assembled weight.</strong> The best available proxy for frame rigidity,
            which is the most immediately perceptible difference between machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Five: weight capacity</strong>, treated as 50 to 75 lbs lower than stated,
            because it is a warranty boundary rather than a comfortable operating point.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Six: subscription position</strong> — specifically what the console does with
            nothing paid, and whether automatic incline control sits behind the paywall.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Seven: warranty terms</strong>, especially the labour period and whether
            service is in-home or return-to-base. Returning a 250 lb machine is not a real remedy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="subscription-question" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Subscription Question</h2>
<p>
            This is the question we would settle first on any modern treadmill, because it changes
            the cost of ownership more than any hardware difference.
          </p>
          <p>
            A subscription at around $39 a month is roughly $2,350 over five years — for most
            machines in the mid-market, more than the treadmill itself cost. Two machines that look
            $500 apart on a comparison page can be $2,850 apart in reality.
          </p>
          <p>
            There are three positions a manufacturer can take, and it is worth establishing which
            applies to the specific model rather than to the brand, because it varies within
            lineups. Some machines have no subscription at all. Some offer optional content while
            retaining full manual speed and incline control unpaid. And some are
            subscription-dependent, where the console does very little without a fee and you have
            paid a premium for a screen you can no longer use.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to subscription-free treadmills</a> covers the five questions to ask a retailer, and what each answer means for the
            five-year figure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-structure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Reading a Warranty Properly</h2>
<p>
            Warranty terms are the most honest specification on any listing, because they are the
            manufacturer stating in writing how long they expect their own components to last. They
            are also routinely misread.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Frame warranties</strong> of ten years or lifetime look generous and cover the
            component least likely to fail. A treadmill frame very rarely breaks.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Parts warranties</strong> cover what does fail — motors and controller boards,
            which are the expensive components and the ones that friction destroys.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Labour warranties</strong> are the ones that matter most and are usually
            shortest, often a single year. Labour is a large share of any repair, and a five-year
            parts term with one year of labour is meaningfully weaker cover than the headline
            suggests.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two clauses are worth finding: whether service is in-home or return-to-base, and
            whether documented maintenance is required. Some manufacturers can decline a motor
            claim on a deck that was clearly never lubricated, which is another reason to keep to
            the <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance schedule</a> and note the dates.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cross-shopping" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What to Cross-Shop Against</h2>
<p>
            Any mid-market treadmill brand should be compared against the alternatives at the same
            price, and the useful comparison is on the specifications above rather than on brand
            reputation.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Against Sole</strong>, if the appeal is buying hardware without an ecosystem
            attached. Sole's position is that machines run with no account and no fee, which makes
            them the reference point for anyone comparing five-year costs. Our <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole brand guide</a> covers that lineup.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Against NordicTrack and ProForm</strong>, if guided content is genuinely
            wanted. Both are built around a content ecosystem, and the honest comparison there is
            subscription-inclusive. Our <a href="/nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack</a> and <a href="/proform-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm</a> guides cover them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Against the used market</strong>, which at this price band buys considerably
            more hardware per dollar — a machine that cost twice as much three to five years ago.
            Our <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new versus used comparison</a> works through when that trade makes sense.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ownership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Ownership Looks Like, Whatever You Buy</h2>
<p>
            The maintenance requirements of a treadmill are essentially brand-independent, and they
            matter more to how long a machine lasts than the badge does. The day-one details are
            not: Horizon is strict about its power supply and safety key, and our guide to <a href="/how-to-start-a-horizon-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to start a Horizon treadmill</a> sets out the start sequence from Horizon’s manuals.
          </p>
          <p>
            The dominant failure mode across every manufacturer is the same: belt-to-deck friction
            rises as the surface dries, the motor draws more current to hold speed, and sustained
            current draw destroys motors and controller boards. Lubricating every 40 to 50 hours of
            use addresses it directly, takes ten minutes, and roughly doubles a machine's life.
          </p>
          <p>
            Check your manual for deck type before applying anything — some decks are pre-waxed and
            are ruined by silicone. Our <a href="/best-treadmill-lubricant/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubricant guide</a> covers identifying which you have, and the <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance pillar</a> covers the full schedule.
          </p>
          <p>
            Expect a belt and deck refresh somewhere around year seven at $250 to $500, which is
            normal wear rather than failure. Our <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance cost guide</a> covers the whole ownership picture.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-is-pending" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What This Page Still Needs</h2>
<p>
            The banner at the top of this page lists what we are verifying before publishing model
            recommendations. We want to set out plainly why those items are held back rather than
            estimated.
          </p>
          <p>
            Specifications in this category change between model years under unchanged product
            names. Motor ratings, deck dimensions, weight capacities and warranty terms all move,
            and a page asserting last year's figures for this year's machine is actively
            misleading to someone about to spend a thousand dollars. Roller diameter in particular
            is rarely published at all, which means an unverified figure is a guess presented as a
            fact.
          </p>
          <p>
            Subscription positions change too, and they change retroactively — a console that
            worked fully unpaid at launch can be altered by a firmware update. That is precisely
            the kind of claim that has to come from current manufacturer documentation rather than
            from inference.
          </p>
          <p>
            Until those are confirmed, this page is excluded from search and carries the draft
            banner. In the meantime, the framework above is genuinely usable: take the seven
            verification points to any retailer and the answers will tell you more than most brand
            pages will.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Use This Page Today</h2>
<p>
            Take the <strong>seven verification points</strong> to the retailer and get the answers
            in writing for the exact model and model year. <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Deck length</a> and width, continuous-duty
            horsepower, roller diameter, assembled weight, weight capacity, subscription position
            and warranty terms.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Settle the subscription question before comparing prices</strong>, because it
            changes five-year cost more than any hardware difference in this price band.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Ask what changed from last year's model</strong>, and if the answer is the
            console, buy the outgoing one.
          </p>
          <p>
            And whatever you buy, <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubricate it every 40 to 50 hours</a>.
            That single habit affects lifespan more than the choice between any two machines at the
            same price.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying Within Any Brand Lineup</h2>
          <p>These apply to any manufacturer, and they are the errors that a brand page asserting unverified specifications would encourage rather than prevent.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming the model number tells you what changed</h3>
              <p>Series prefixes and tier numbers indicate position within a family, not whether two machines share a frame, motor or deck. Ask specifically which components differ between two models before paying for the higher number — it is often the console and very little else.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying for the current model year without asking what changed</h3>
              <p>Annual refreshes are overwhelmingly console and software updates on carried-over hardware. That makes the outgoing model frequently the best value in any lineup. Ask the retailer directly what differs, and treat an evasive answer as confirmation that the difference is the screen.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Comparing prices across retailers without checking the model variant</h3>
              <p>The same chassis is often sold under different model numbers at different retailers, sometimes with a genuine specification difference and often without. This is the most common reason two apparently identical machines carry different prices, and it is resolved by comparing the actual specifications rather than the numbers.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Reading the frame warranty as the warranty</h3>
              <p>Ten-year and lifetime frame cover protects the component least likely to fail. What matters is the parts term, the labour term — usually the shortest — and whether service is in-home or return-to-base. Shipping a 250 lb treadmill back to a manufacturer is not a remedy anyone actually uses.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            This page is an editorial draft. The <strong class="text-white">seven verification
            points</strong> above are usable today — take them to any retailer and get the answers
            in writing for the exact model and model year you are considering.
          </p>
          <p>
            Model-specific figures are held back deliberately rather than estimated, because
            specifications in this category change between model years under unchanged names. Until
            they are confirmed, the <a href="/treadmill-buying-guide-2026/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">buying guide</a> and the <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">subscription guide</a> will get you further than any brand page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="ed-more not-prose">
          <div class="ed-more-k">Keep reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/sole-fitness/">Sole Fitness Treadmills</a></li>
            <li><a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/">Best Treadmills Without Subscriptions</a></li>
          </ul>
        </aside>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Why does this page not list Horizon models and prices?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Because we have not verified them. Treadmill specifications change between model years under unchanged product names, and a page asserting unverified motor ratings, deck sizes or warranty terms reads as authoritative while being wrong. The banner at the top lists exactly what is being confirmed before publication.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What should I check before buying any Horizon treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Seven things, in writing, for the exact model and model year: deck length and width, continuous-duty horsepower (not peak), roller diameter, assembled weight, weight capacity, what the console does with no subscription active, and the warranty terms — particularly the labour period and whether service is in-home.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is it better to buy last year's treadmill model?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Frequently yes. Annual refreshes in this category are overwhelmingly console and software changes while the frame, motor, deck and rollers carry over unchanged, so the outgoing model is often mechanically identical for several hundred dollars less. Ask the retailer what changed, and treat a vague answer as meaning the screen.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much does a treadmill subscription add to the cost?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              At around $39 a month it is roughly $470 a year and about $2,350 over five years — for most mid-market machines, more than the treadmill cost. Establish what the console does with nothing paid before comparing purchase prices, because two machines $500 apart can be $2,850 apart in reality.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What warranty terms actually matter on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The parts and labour periods, not the frame. Frames very rarely fail; motors and controller boards do, and labour is a large share of any repair. A five-year parts term with one year of labour is weaker than the headline suggests. Also check whether service is in-home, since returning a 250 lb machine is not a real remedy.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does the brand matter for treadmill maintenance?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Barely. The dominant failure mode is identical across manufacturers: belt-to-deck friction rises, the motor draws more current, and sustained draw destroys motors and boards. Lubricating every 40 to 50 hours addresses it on any machine — check the manual first, since some decks are pre-waxed and are ruined by silicone.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
          <div class="author-card">
            <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="56" height="56" loading="lazy" />
            <div>
              <div class="ac-k">Written by</div>
              <div class="ac-n"><a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a></div>
              <p class="ac-b">
